Built into the file by every build
---------------------------------

  Archivo                      OFL-1.1  Copyright 2020 The Archivo Project
                                        Authors
                                        https://github.com/Omnibus-Type/Archivo
  JetBrains Mono               OFL-1.1  Copyright 2020 The JetBrains Mono
                                        Project Authors
                                        https://github.com/JetBrains/JetBrainsMono

Both are embedded as woff2 data URIs — the latin subset, one variable file per
family — because a `<link>` to Google Fonts meant the tool rendered in its own
typography with a connection and in the machine's fallback without one, and
because the offline build cannot reach a CDN at all. About 100 kB of the
output.

The SIL Open Font License requires the copyright notice and the licence to
accompany any copy of the font software, and this artifact contains the font
software, so `build.js` puts both notices and the licence text into the banner
at the top of `dfm-tool.html`. The licence body is identical for the two
families, so it appears once with both notices above it. The upstream licence
files are in `src/fonts/`, and `src/fonts/README.md` records where the woff2
files came from and how to refresh them. Neither family declares a Reserved
Font Name, so a subset keeping the family name is within the licence.

`npm run test:smoke` asserts both faces actually load and that nothing was
requested from a font host; `npm run test:offline` asserts no off-origin
request is made at all.

Built into the file by `node build.js --vendor`
----------------------------------------------

three.js and jsPDF are embedded in the output. Both are MIT, which requires the
copyright notice to be included with the distribution; both minified builds
carry their own `@license` header and those headers are embedded verbatim, so
the notices travel with the artifact. `npm run test:offline` asserts they are
still present.

The OpenCascade reader is never embedded — it is 6 MB and only a STEP import
needs it — so STEP support remains network-dependent in every build.

